    <h2>How this calculation works</h2>
    <p>When an option pool top-up is created <em>pre-money</em>, the target pool percentage and the investor's percentage are both carved out of the post-money cap table first — and whatever is left belongs to founders and existing shareholders. In formula terms: Investor % = Investment &divide; (Pre-money + Investment); Founders' resulting % = 100% &minus; Target Pool % &minus; Investor %. The "extra" dilution founders absorb, beyond the investment itself, is the gap between the target pool and the pool that already existed before the round.</p>

    <h2>Why this matters at term sheet stage, not after</h2>
    <p>The pool size and whether it is created pre-money or post-money is a negotiated term, not a fixed rule. Founders who understand this arithmetic before a term sheet arrives are in a materially better position to negotiate a smaller top-up, a later top-up, or a post-money mechanic — than founders discovering the effect for the first time in the definitive documents. Read the full context in <a href="/legal-insights/esop-pool-sizing-for-startups/">ESOP pool sizing: what to decide before you need it</a>.</p>

    <p class="mt-32" style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text-muted);border-top:1px solid var(--border);padding-top:24px">This calculator is an illustrative estimate using a simplified, standard pre-money pool-shuffle formula. It does not account for multiple share classes, liquidation preferences, convertible instruments already on the cap table, rounding conventions, or deal-specific mechanics — and does not constitute legal or financial advice. For an actual cap table model for your round, <a href="/contact/">talk to the chambers</a>.</p>
